# Deliberately empty of rules. Read this before adding one.
#
# This file used to contain the standard single-page-app catch-all:
#
#     /*    /index.html   200
#
# That is correct on Netlify and it BREAKS THE ENTIRE APP on Cloudflare
# Pages, which is where this now deploys.
#
# The difference is the order. Netlify serves a matching static asset first
# and only falls through to the redirect when nothing matched. Cloudflare
# applies redirects BEFORE looking for an asset, and its own documentation
# is explicit that they are "always followed, regardless of whether or not
# an asset matches the incoming request".
#
# So on Cloudflare that one line rewrites main.dart.js, canvaskit.wasm and
# all 376 figures to index.html. The page loads, the browser asks for the
# app, gets a copy of the HTML back, and nothing runs. It fails in a way
# that looks like a broken build rather than a routing rule.
#
# WHAT REPLACES IT
#
# Nothing, and it turns out nothing needed to.
#
# This app has no path routing at all. Every link it produces is a query
# string on the root — /?report=<token>, /?consent=<token>, /?pay=<token> —
# and lib/main.dart reads them with Uri.base.queryParameters and nothing
# else. There is no router, no onGenerateRoute, no path a browser could ask
# for that is not either the root or a real file.
#
# A query string never reaches the server as a path. /?consent=abc is a
# request for / with a query attached, so it was always going to be served
# index.html by any host on earth. The catch-all was defensive rather than
# load-bearing, which is why removing it costs nothing.
#
# Cloudflare Pages would cover a path route anyway: a project with no
# top-level 404.html is treated as a single-page app and unmatched paths
# get index.html. That is now belt-and-braces rather than the thing holding
# the links up.
#
# THE TRAP THAT TURNS IT OFF
#
# Adding web/404.html. The moment a top-level 404.html exists, Cloudflare
# stops assuming this is an SPA and starts serving that file for unmatched
# paths instead — and every deep link 404s. A custom 404 page is the one
# obvious improvement that would silently break sharing.
#
# If this ever moves back to Netlify, put the line at the top back.
#
# https://developers.cloudflare.com/pages/configuration/serving-pages/
# https://developers.cloudflare.com/pages/configuration/redirects/
